937	/**
938	 * Per-domain SPF / DKIM / DMARC / MX self-check.
939	 *
940	 * WHY: "my mail goes to spam" was undiagnosable from inside the product. The
941	 * platform owner hit exactly this and had to diagnose it with an external
942	 * third-party tool, so every customer in the same position had no route at all.
943	 *
944	 * Two things this panel must never do, because both would make it the codebase's
945	 * recurring defect in a new place — a surface reporting an outcome it never
946	 * verified, here telling someone their mail is fine while it goes to spam:
947	 *
948	 *   1. Report a status the server did not derive from DNS. Every badge below
949	 *      comes from `checkEmailDeliverability`, which reads the zone's own
950	 *      AUTHORITATIVE nameservers (this host's `hosts: files myhostname dns`
951	 *      nsswitch order can answer a lookup out of /etc/hosts while looking like a
952	 *      real query — that produced one confidently wrong diagnosis already).
953	 *   2. Round a partial answer up. `unknown` renders as its own slate state, not
954	 *      as a pass, and the nameservers actually queried are printed so the result
955	 *      is auditable rather than taken on trust.
956	 *
957	 * Not auto-run: each check is several outbound DNS queries plus one Mailcow call,
958	 * and the customer has to be looking at it for the answer to be useful.
959	 */
960	function DeliverabilityPanel({
961	  subscriptionId,
962	  domainName,
963	}: {
